#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203702 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203912 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04206f72-e018-5925-a58f-179e897595dc" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Existence of a Multiplicative Inverse"}] :question/id #uuid "b0bec0e5-41ab-591f-ab80-5e43602ff808" :question/math-academy-id "e-14656" :question/problem "Which of the following integers has a multiplicative inverse modulo $25?$\n\n- $7$\n\n- $10$\n\n- $12$" :question/worked-solution "An integer $a$ has a multiplicative inverse modulo $n$ if and only if $a$ and $n$ are coprime, i.e., $\\textrm{gcd}(a,n)=1.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- $7$ has a multiplicative inverse modulo $25$ since $\\textrm{gcd}(7,25)=1. \\:\\:{\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark}$\n\n- $10$ does *not* have a multiplicative inverse modulo $25$ since $\\textrm{gcd}(10,25)=5\\neq1. \\:\\:{\\color{red}\\times}$\n\n- $12$ has a multiplicative inverse modulo $25$ since $\\textrm{gcd}(12,25)=1. \\:\\:{\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203703 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203913 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d467de67-1ec6-57ab-98e7-8f7e9797d54d" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Inverses Using Exponentiation"}] :question/id #uuid "060283e9-0fbd-523b-bc30-37936fabacf5" :question/math-academy-id "e-14657" :question/problem "Use trial and error to compute the multiplicative inverse of $8$ modulo $15.$" :question/worked-solution "The multiplicative inverse of $8$ modulo $15,$ denoted $8^{-1},$ is the number $x$ such that\n\n$$\n8 \\cdot x \\equiv 1 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\:15).\n$$\n\nNote that since $\\text{gcd}(8,15) = 1,$ an inverse element exists and is unique up to congruence modulo $15.$\n\nSince the modulus $n=15$ is small, we can find the inverse $x$ by computing exponents of $8$ (modulo $15$) until we get a result of $1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{8}^{2} & ≡8\\cdot 8≡64≡4\\cdot 15+4≡4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\ {8}^{3} & ≡8\\cdot 4≡32≡2\\cdot 15+2≡2 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\ {8}^{4} & ≡8\\cdot 2≡16≡15+1≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,15)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $8 \\cdot 2 \\equiv 1 \\, (\\textrm{mod} \\:15).$ Therefore, the multiplicative inverse of $8$ modulo $15$ is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203704 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203914 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc98cf4e-55f1-5fbd-a779-fb400adf1b44" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Inverses Using the Extended Euclidean Algorithm"}] :question/id #uuid "15204e14-a2a1-5591-8448-1864066a29b2" :question/math-academy-id "e-14658" :question/problem "Use the extended Euclidean algorithm to compute the multiplicative inverse of $15$ modulo $86.$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, notice that $\\textrm{gcd}(15,86)=1.$ So, the multiplicative inverse of $15$ modulo $86$ exists.\n\nThe multiplicative inverse of $15$ modulo $86$ is the number $x$ such that\n\n$$\n15\\cdot x \\equiv 1 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\:86).\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $x,$ we will use the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}86 & = & 15\\cdot 5 & + & 11 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 15 & = & 11\\cdot 1 & + & 4 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 11 & = & 4\\cdot 2 & + & 3 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 4 & = & 3\\cdot 1 & + & 1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving for the rightmost terms in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11 & =86-15\\cdot 5 \\\\ 4 & =15-11 \\\\ 3 & =11-4\\cdot 2 \\\\ 1 & =4-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =4-3 \\\\  & =4-(11-4\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 3-11 \\\\  & =(15-11)\\cdot 3-11 \\\\  & =15\\cdot 3-11\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =15\\cdot 3-(86-15\\cdot 5)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =15\\cdot 23-86\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =15\\cdot 23+86\\cdot (-4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write this result modulo $86,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15\\cdot 23+86\\cdot (-4) & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,86) \\\\ 15\\cdot 23 & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,86)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $15\\cdot 23\\equiv 1 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:86),$ which means that $15^{-1} \\: \\textrm{mod} \\: 86=\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}23}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415620 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203912 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04206f72-e018-5925-a58f-179e897595dc" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Existence of a Multiplicative Inverse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a41fc355-034f-5ded-bdfb-ebfb582a1bda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415595} {:answer/id #uuid "b03c9ec0-7f75-5d97-abbd-94b2869f47ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415596} {:answer/id #uuid "b0c4fbb0-762c-5b67-afd1-1fb5e5110f8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415597} {:answer/id #uuid "d07425cc-ed1f-5b0a-8751-4e3973904462" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415600} {:answer/id #uuid "d66363e6-df16-5391-b2d0-6a090267e3a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415601}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d07425cc-ed1f-5b0a-8751-4e3973904462" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415600} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0aef841-96f7-59bb-8f67-62f78c7ca5b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415612}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6d34f2bd-1406-50c4-9338-867f1ecca34e" :question/math-academy-id "q-213405" :question/problem "Which of the following integers has a multiplicative inverse modulo $36?$\n\n- $5$\n- $13$\n- $24$" :question/worked-solution "An integer $a$ has a multiplicative inverse modulo $n$ if and only if $a$ and $n$ are coprime, i.e., $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- $5$ has a multiplicative inverse modulo $36$ since $\\text{gcd}(5,36)=1.\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $13$ has a multiplicative inverse modulo $36$ since $\\text{gcd}(13,36)=1.\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $24$ does *not* have a multiplicative inverse modulo $36$ since $\\text{gcd}(24,36)=12\\ne 1.\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415621 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203912 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04206f72-e018-5925-a58f-179e897595dc" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Existence of a Multiplicative Inverse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "161735ec-56d4-5072-8a75-665310f43f19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415586} {:answer/id #uuid "4c5e7ff7-af02-5c83-9cc7-4a20cdd2b462" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415587} {:answer/id #uuid "7aad8420-3c48-5474-98cb-d9e00f367a26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415592} {:answer/id #uuid "b51e64bf-376c-52bb-ac74-2778610f3e9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415598} {:answer/id #uuid "f3f743a1-b3bd-5c44-85bc-086b14acdf8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415603}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7aad8420-3c48-5474-98cb-d9e00f367a26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415592} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9802078-fa7f-52a5-a1e5-14125231ee58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415610}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "af0eb712-6f36-542d-b37c-e07c5fedf4a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-213407" :question/problem "Which of the following integers has a multiplicative inverse modulo $27?$\n\n- $11$\n- $16$\n- $23$" :question/worked-solution "An integer $a$ has a multiplicative inverse modulo $n$ if and only if $a$ and $n$ are coprime, i.e., $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- $11$ has a multiplicative inverse modulo $27$ since $\\text{gcd}(11,27)=1.\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $16$ has a multiplicative inverse modulo $27$ since $\\text{gcd}(16,27)=1.\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $23$ has a multiplicative inverse modulo $27$ since $\\text{gcd}(23,27)=1.\\,\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415619 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203913 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d467de67-1ec6-57ab-98e7-8f7e9797d54d" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Inverses Using Exponentiation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5b0044e-5976-528d-9010-0418652be505" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415599}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5b0044e-5976-528d-9010-0418652be505" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415599} :answer-field/id #uuid "df897945-d81d-5243-b778-8cc8364680a5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415614}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "675f6fdb-8d6a-5e1a-8484-30f4c73cb80a" :question/math-academy-id "q-213414" :question/problem "Use trial and error to compute the following residue.\n\n${3}^{-1}\\,\\text{mod}\\,5=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The multiplicative inverse of $3$ modulo $5,$ denoted ${3}^{-1},$ is the number $x$ such that\n\n$$\n3\\cdot x≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5).\n$$\n\nNote that since $\\text{gcd}(3,5)=1,$ an inverse element exists and is unique up to congruence modulo $5.$\n\nSince the modulus $n=5$ is small, we can find the inverse $x$ by computing exponents of $3$ (modulo $5$) until we get a result of $1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2} & ≡3\\cdot 3≡9≡5+4≡4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ {3}^{3} & ≡3\\cdot 4≡12≡2\\cdot 5+2≡2 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ {3}^{4} & ≡3\\cdot 2≡6≡5+1≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $3\\cdot 2≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5).$ Therefore, the multiplicative inverse of $3$ modulo $5$ is $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415618 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203913 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d467de67-1ec6-57ab-98e7-8f7e9797d54d" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Inverses Using Exponentiation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92df733c-9e8a-5b23-a107-168e6eed32b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415594}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92df733c-9e8a-5b23-a107-168e6eed32b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415594} :answer-field/id #uuid "25bf507a-8609-584a-8895-2544c9926a42" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415605}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4915f9fb-f03b-5b7e-8a32-e3e0683d3cad" :question/math-academy-id "q-213441" :question/problem "Use trial and error to compute the following residue.\n\n${3}^{-1}\\,\\text{mod}\\,11=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The multiplicative inverse of $3$ modulo $11,$ denoted ${3}^{-1},$ is the number $x$ such that\n\n$$\n3\\cdot x≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11).\n$$\n\nNote that since $\\text{gcd}(3,11)=1,$ an inverse element exists and is unique up to congruence modulo $11.$\n\nSince the modulus $n=11$ is small, we can find the inverse $x$ by computing exponents of $3$ (modulo $11$) until we get a result of $1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2} & ≡3\\cdot 3≡9 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ {3}^{3} & ≡3\\cdot 9≡27≡2\\cdot 11+5≡5 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ {3}^{4} & ≡3\\cdot 5≡15≡11+4≡4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ {3}^{5} & ≡3\\cdot 4≡12≡11+1≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $3\\cdot 4≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11).$ Therefore, the multiplicative inverse of $3$ modulo $11$ is $4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415616 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203914 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc98cf4e-55f1-5fbd-a779-fb400adf1b44" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Inverses Using the Extended Euclidean Algorithm"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67ddaadf-b1b7-5b7d-8385-f1f708518b6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415591}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67ddaadf-b1b7-5b7d-8385-f1f708518b6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415591} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a797595-f7b3-5fbe-8e81-bec203cecb7b" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415604} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2f7e142-5fde-519c-99ea-9b33ca10f2d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "61" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415602}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2f7e142-5fde-519c-99ea-9b33ca10f2d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "61" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415602} :answer-field/id #uuid "93f5dfb3-b0bd-54d4-9f0c-3a23cd8eb0e9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415608} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7dd57479-5967-51e1-bdfa-54df7817cc48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "61" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415593}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7dd57479-5967-51e1-bdfa-54df7817cc48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "61" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415593} :answer-field/id #uuid "a57d0469-969b-5d66-a222-054cf955e5d0" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415609} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "104e7c32-f9c9-51bc-ae0f-4c410215746f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415584}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "104e7c32-f9c9-51bc-ae0f-4c410215746f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415584} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd8ed543-c520-541d-aaca-a83f9d308e3e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415613}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "18a6f0f2-d5f2-58c9-b7c0-478b95c5376b" :question/math-academy-id "q-213530" :question/problem "Fill in the blanks to make the following reasoning true.\n\nSince $\\text{gcd}(55,258)=$\n{{field-1}}, \nby the extended Euclidean algorithm, we have \n\n| $1\\,=\\,55\\,\\cdot ($\n{{field-2}}\n$)+\\,258\\,\\cdot ($\n{{field-3}}\n$).$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore, ${55}^{-1}\\,\\text{mod}\\,258=$\n{{field-4}} (expressed as the smallest nonnegative residue)." :question/worked-solution "First of all, notice that $\\text{gcd}(55,258)=1.$ So, the multiplicative inverse of $55$ modulo $258$ exists.\n\nThe multiplicative inverse of $55$ modulo $258$ is the number $x$ such that\n\n$$\n55\\cdot x≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,258).\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $x,$ we will use the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}258 & = & 55\\cdot 4 & + & 38 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 55 & = & 38\\cdot 1 & + & 17 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 38 & = & 17\\cdot 2 & + & 4 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 17 & = & 4\\cdot 4 & + & 1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving for the rightmost terms in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}38 & =258-55\\cdot 4 \\\\ 17 & =55-38 \\\\ 4 & =38-17\\cdot 2 \\\\ 1 & =17-4\\cdot 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =17-4\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =17-(38-17\\cdot 2)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =17\\cdot 9-38\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =(55-38)\\cdot 9-38\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =55\\cdot 9-38\\cdot 13 \\\\  & =55\\cdot 9-(258-55\\cdot 4)\\cdot 13 \\\\  & =55\\cdot 61+258\\cdot (-13)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write this result modulo $258,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}55\\cdot 61+258\\cdot (-13) & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,258) \\\\ 55\\cdot 61 & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,258)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $55\\cdot 61≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,258),$ which means that ${55}^{-1}\\,\\text{mod}\\,258=61.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415617 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203914 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc98cf4e-55f1-5fbd-a779-fb400adf1b44" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Inverses Using the Extended Euclidean Algorithm"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60a83021-eb0b-5bd6-adbb-9205cb87d642" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415590}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60a83021-eb0b-5bd6-adbb-9205cb87d642" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415590} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a0ab631-1b27-5aa9-aef1-a92dc676b3f2" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415606} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "160d5f45-9189-5260-9c2f-7a57f82aad3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415585}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "160d5f45-9189-5260-9c2f-7a57f82aad3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415585} :answer-field/id #uuid "89c3c4d5-f51f-5d05-ab6e-1c068b6cd4f9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415607} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b74eb17-4d64-5719-a0ec-bfc1de3209b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415589}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b74eb17-4d64-5719-a0ec-bfc1de3209b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415589} :answer-field/id #uuid "af0eb2c2-9bb3-53f5-8685-d1b8e9d768b7" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415611} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5539c33b-1dce-5393-93a1-4064a290b70d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415588}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5539c33b-1dce-5393-93a1-4064a290b70d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415588} :answer-field/id #uuid "e85832e1-b55f-56ed-bf6f-47527d9704c8" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415615}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3e2bafbf-e373-5f9a-9cd6-8fbc664e6471" :question/math-academy-id "q-213532" :question/problem "Fill in the blanks to make the following reasoning true.\n\nSince $\\text{gcd}(50,97)=$\n{{field-1}}, \nby the extended Euclidean algorithm, we have \n\n| $1\\,=\\,50\\,\\cdot ($\n{{field-2}}\n$)+\\,97\\,\\cdot ($\n{{field-3}}\n$).$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore, ${50}^{-1}\\,\\text{mod}\\,97=$\n{{field-4}} (expressed as the smallest nonnegative residue)." :question/worked-solution "First of all, notice that $\\text{gcd}(50,97)=1.$ So, the multiplicative inverse of $50$ modulo $97$ exists.\n\nThe multiplicative inverse of $50$ modulo $97$ is the number $x$ such that\n\n$$\n50\\cdot x≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,97).\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $x,$ we will use the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}97 & = & 50\\cdot 1 & + & 47 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 50 & = & 47\\cdot 1 & + & 3 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 47 & = & 3\\cdot 15 & + & 2 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 3 & = & 2\\cdot 1 & + & 1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving for the rightmost terms in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}47 & =97-50 \\\\ 3 & =50-47 \\\\ 2 & =47-3\\cdot 15 \\\\ 1 & =3-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =3-2 \\\\  & =3-(47-3\\cdot 15) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 16-47 \\\\  & =(50-47)\\cdot 16-47 \\\\  & =50\\cdot 16-47\\cdot 17 \\\\  & =50\\cdot 16-(97-50)\\cdot 17 \\\\  & =50\\cdot 33-97\\cdot 17 \\\\  & =50\\cdot 33+97\\cdot (-17)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write this result modulo $97,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}50\\cdot 33+97\\cdot (-17) & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,97) \\\\ 50\\cdot 33 & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,97)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $50\\cdot 33≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,97),$ which means that ${50}^{-1}\\,\\text{mod}\\,97=33.$"}]}
